    {
      "id": "p0122-b026",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "A monster named \"Icecap\" sometimes appears in battle. He wears a hat made of ice that he considers his pride and joy. In Japanese, Icecap’s name became hyōbō - hyō means \"ice\", while bō is a play on the Japanese words for \"cap\" and \"boy\". This is an interesting, rare example of a name that works perfectly in a straightforward translation. Not only that, the Japanese name naturally contains an extra layer of wordplay not found in the English version.",
        "raw": "A monster named \"Icecap\" sometimes appears in battle. He wears a hat made\nof ice that he considers his pride and joy. In Japanese, Icecap's name became\nhy6b6 - hyo means \"ice\", while bo is a play on the Japanese words for \"cap\"\nand \"boy\". This is an interesting, rare example of a name that works perfectly\nin a straightforward translation. Not only that, the Japanese name naturally\ncontains an extra layer of wordplay not found in the English version.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.29334607,
          0.80688571,
          0.88857357,
          0.91045
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.28893087,
          0.80617143,
          0.89627473,
          0.91152143
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          44,
          45,
          46,
          47,
          48,
          49
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "战斗中有时会出现名叫“Icecap”的怪物。他戴着一顶冰做的帽子，对此引以为傲、爱不释手。日语中，Icecap 的名字变成了 hyōbō：hyō 是“冰”，bō 则同时指“帽子”和“小子”。这是个有趣而少见的例子，名字直译就能完美成立。不仅如此，日文名还自然地多出了一层英语版没有的双关。",
        "status": "draft",
